# macOS Keychain Provider

The `macoskeychain` provider reads passwords from the macOS Keychain as
[ephemeral values](https://opentofu.org/docs/language/ephemerality/): OpenTofu
and Terraform hand them to the configuration that needs them, such as a provider
block or a write-only argument, and never write them to a plan or state file.

It offers only ephemeral resources. It has no managed resources and no data
sources, because both store their values in the state.

It needs OpenTofu 1.11 or later, or Terraform 1.10 or later, running on macOS.
On any other platform the provider still installs, so `init` and `validate`
work in mixed teams, but every ephemeral resource fails with an error that
explains why.

## Example Usage

{{ tffile "examples/provider/provider.tf" }}

Store a secret once from a terminal. With `-w` at the end, `security` prompts
for the value, so it never lands in your shell history:

```shell
security add-generic-password -s sentry-auth-token -a deploy -w
```

Then read it where it is needed:

```terraform
ephemeral "macoskeychain_generic_password" "sentry" {
  service = "sentry-auth-token"
  account = "deploy"
}

provider "sentry" {
  token = ephemeral.macoskeychain_generic_password.sentry.password
}
```

## Security model

- **Nothing reaches the plan or the state.** Every value the provider returns
  is ephemeral, and the `password` attribute is also marked sensitive. OpenTofu
  rejects an ephemeral value in any argument that would be stored.
- **The secret travels only through a pipe.** The provider reads the item by
  running `/usr/bin/security find-generic-password` (or
  `find-internet-password`), by absolute path, and reads the password from that
  child process's output. The secret never appears on a command line or in an
  environment, and the provider never logs it. Error messages name only the
  service or server, the account, the keychain path and the exit status of
  `security`, never its output.
- **Read only.** The provider never creates, changes or deletes keychain items.
- **macOS decides who may read an item.** Each item has an access control list
  of trusted applications. The provider does not bypass it; `security` reads
  the item on its behalf. See below.

The provider does not protect a secret from other code running as your user
that the item's access control trusts, and the secret is in the memory of
OpenTofu and of every provider that receives it.

## Granting access to an item

An item created with `security add-generic-password` (or
`add-internet-password`) and no `-T` or `-A` option trusts only
`/usr/bin/security`. The provider reads items through that same
`/usr/bin/security`, so it reads them without any macOS dialog: at plan and at
apply, in SSH sessions once the keychain is unlocked, and after every provider
upgrade. Store secrets that way and there is nothing to grant.

An item created by another application, such as Keychain Access or a browser,
may not trust `/usr/bin/security`. The first time the provider reads it, macOS
asks whether `security` may use it; choose **Always Allow** so it does not ask
again. Because the trusted program is `/usr/bin/security` and not the provider
binary, the answer survives provider upgrades. When no dialog can be shown (an
SSH session, CI), the read fails with an error instead: read the item once from
a logged-in desktop session, or create it again with `security`.

A locked keychain needs its password. In a desktop session macOS asks for it;
in an SSH session, unlock the keychain first with `security unlock-keychain`.

## Common surprises

### Migrating a write-only argument plans an update with nothing changed

**Symptom.** A write-only argument used to receive a variable that was
`ephemeral` but not `sensitive`. Once it receives `password` instead, `tofu plan`
proposes an update for every resource that takes it, with no attribute that
differs between `before` and `after`. In `tofu show -json`, measured on
`secret_data_wo` of `google_secret_manager_secret_version`, only the sensitivity
changes:

```text
before migrating  actions=[no-op]   after_sensitive={secret_data:true}
after migrating   actions=[update]  after_sensitive={secret_data:true, secret_data_wo:true}
```

**Cause.** `password` is sensitive, so the argument now receives a marked value
that the state does not record as marked, and OpenTofu plans that difference as
an update. A value passed only to a `provider` block does not do this, because
provider blocks have no state.

**Way out.** Drop the mark at that argument with `nonsensitive()`, and the plan
goes back to no changes:

```terraform
resource "google_secret_manager_secret_version" "database" {
  secret                 = google_secret_manager_secret.database.id
  secret_data_wo         = nonsensitive(ephemeral.macoskeychain_generic_password.database.password)
  secret_data_wo_version = 1
}
```

The value stays ephemeral, so it still reaches neither the plan nor the state,
and `scripts/prove-no-secret-in-state.sh` checks that it arrives intact after an
apply. `nonsensitive()` removes only what hides a value in CLI output, so use it
on write-only arguments and nowhere else. `password` itself stays sensitive.

### `tofu test` without `mock_provider` reads the real Keychain

**Symptom.** A test run that plans a configuration with these ephemeral
resources opens them for real, unless the file mocks the provider. Measured with
a keychain path that does not exist: without a mock, one read and the error
`Keychain file not found`; with a mock, no reads.

**Way out.** Put this in every test file whose runs use such a configuration:

```hcl
mock_provider "macoskeychain" {}
```

A file whose runs all test modules that do not use this provider
(`module { source = … }` in every run) cannot have it: there `mock_provider` fails
with `Missing required provider`. Such a file stays off the Keychain only while
every one of its runs has a `module` block, and nothing enforces that.

